-- Additional Year 10 question-bank content: 40 Humanities and 64 Mathematics & Science
-- questions (each page is a complete stimulus group) plus 24 Written Expression topics.
--
-- * Rows are bank rows (is_bank = true, session_id null), so the existing assessment code serves them
--   exactly like the other bank content. No application code changes are needed.
-- * Safe to re-run: a row is only inserted when its id does not already exist, so edits made in the
--   admin question-bank editor are never overwritten.
-- * answer_options is written in whichever form the column uses (jsonb, json or a text array).

drop table if exists pg_temp.new_question_bank_rows;
create temporary table new_question_bank_rows (
  id text,
  section text,
  difficulty text,
  time integer,
  question_text text,
  answer_options jsonb,
  correct_answer integer,
  explanation text,
  passage text,
  stimulus_group text
);

insert into new_question_bank_rows
  (id, section, difficulty, time, question_text, answer_options, correct_answer, explanation, passage, stimulus_group)
values
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P01-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'What does Priya’s decision to put her cards down suggest?', '["She has chosen to speak honestly rather than recite.","She has forgotten what her speech was meant to say.","She hopes the audience will leave the hall early.","She is angry with the person who adjusted the microphone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Priya replaces the rehearsed speech with something she wrote the night before, which suggests she wants to speak naturally.', 'Priya had practised the speech so often that the words no longer felt like words. In the school hall, the microphone squealed as she adjusted it, and a ripple of laughter passed through the front rows. She glanced at her notes, then at the clock above the stage, and put the cards down on the lectern. “I wrote something else last night,” she said, her voice steadier than she expected. “It’s shorter.” Someone near the back stopped whispering.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P01-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which detail best shows that the audience’s attitude is changing?', '["The microphone squealed as she adjusted it.","Someone near the back stopped whispering.","A ripple of laughter passed through the front rows.","She glanced at the clock above the stage."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The whispering stops once Priya begins to speak differently, showing the audience is starting to listen.', 'Priya had practised the speech so often that the words no longer felt like words. In the school hall, the microphone squealed as she adjusted it, and a ripple of laughter passed through the front rows. She glanced at her notes, then at the clock above the stage, and put the cards down on the lectern. “I wrote something else last night,” she said, her voice steadier than she expected. “It’s shorter.” Someone near the back stopped whispering.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P01-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'The phrase “the words no longer felt like words” most nearly suggests that', '["the speech had been written in another language","Priya disliked the subject she was speaking about","repeating it so often had drained it of meaning","the words were too difficult for her to pronounce"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Practising a speech until it is automatic can drain it of meaning, so the words become sounds rather than ideas.', 'Priya had practised the speech so often that the words no longer felt like words. In the school hall, the microphone squealed as she adjusted it, and a ripple of laughter passed through the front rows. She glanced at her notes, then at the clock above the stage, and put the cards down on the lectern. “I wrote something else last night,” she said, her voice steadier than she expected. “It’s shorter.” Someone near the back stopped whispering.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P01-Q4', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the effect of the description “her voice steadier than she expected”?', '["It shows the microphone is now working properly.","It proves she is still reading closely from her notes.","It suggests the audience has stopped paying attention.","It shows her confidence growing as she takes charge."]'::jsonb, 3, 'She expected to sound nervous, but acting on her own decision steadies her.', 'Priya had practised the speech so often that the words no longer felt like words. In the school hall, the microphone squealed as she adjusted it, and a ripple of laughter passed through the front rows. She glanced at her notes, then at the clock above the stage, and put the cards down on the lectern. “I wrote something else last night,” she said, her voice steadier than she expected. “It’s shorter.” Someone near the back stopped whispering.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P01-Q5', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which word best describes the mood of the room at the end of the extract?', '["Expectant","Bored","Hostile","Careless"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The audience falls quiet and waits to hear what Priya will say next.', 'Priya had practised the speech so often that the words no longer felt like words. In the school hall, the microphone squealed as she adjusted it, and a ripple of laughter passed through the front rows. She glanced at her notes, then at the clock above the stage, and put the cards down on the lectern. “I wrote something else last night,” she said, her voice steadier than she expected. “It’s shorter.” Someone near the back stopped whispering.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P02-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'What is the writer’s main claim?', '["Landfill sites should be closed down across the whole region.","Leaves should be composted locally, not trucked to landfill.","Gardens no longer need soil to grow plants.","The town should plant fewer trees along its streets."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The passage argues for local composting as a better alternative to transporting leaves to landfill.', 'Every autumn, our town sweeps thousands of tonnes of fallen leaves into trucks and drives them to a landfill site forty kilometres away. This is a costly habit with a simple alternative. If leaves were collected at the park depots and composted locally, the council could return rich soil to the same gardens that lose their leaves each year. Critics say composting needs space and attracts pests, but the depot at Millbrook has operated for six years without a single recorded complaint. Surely a practice that saves money, reduces transport and rebuilds soil deserves a fair trial in every suburb.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P02-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which evidence does the writer use to answer the critics?', '["The landfill site is forty kilometres away from the town centre.","Leaves fall from the trees every autumn.","Millbrook’s depot has run for six years without complaint.","Compost can be used to make rich soil."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Millbrook record directly answers the claim that composting depots cause problems.', 'Every autumn, our town sweeps thousands of tonnes of fallen leaves into trucks and drives them to a landfill site forty kilometres away. This is a costly habit with a simple alternative. If leaves were collected at the park depots and composted locally, the council could return rich soil to the same gardens that lose their leaves each year. Critics say composting needs space and attracts pests, but the depot at Millbrook has operated for six years without a single recorded complaint. Surely a practice that saves money, reduces transport and rebuilds soil deserves a fair trial in every suburb.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P02-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'The word “Surely” in the final sentence mainly serves to', '["show that the writer is unsure whether the argument works","introduce a completely new topic","report a fact taken from a council document","present the conclusion as obvious to any sensible reader"]'::jsonb, 3, '“Surely” invites agreement by treating the conclusion as common sense.', 'Every autumn, our town sweeps thousands of tonnes of fallen leaves into trucks and drives them to a landfill site forty kilometres away. This is a costly habit with a simple alternative. If leaves were collected at the park depots and composted locally, the council could return rich soil to the same gardens that lose their leaves each year. Critics say composting needs space and attracts pests, but the depot at Millbrook has operated for six years without a single recorded complaint. Surely a practice that saves money, reduces transport and rebuilds soil deserves a fair trial in every suburb.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P02-Q4', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which assumption does the argument rely on?', '["A result at one depot would carry over to other suburbs.","Composting needs no extra space or planning at all.","Critics are never correct about anything in this debate.","Leaves are the only waste the town produces."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The writer uses one successful depot to argue for a trial everywhere, which assumes the result would carry over.', 'Every autumn, our town sweeps thousands of tonnes of fallen leaves into trucks and drives them to a landfill site forty kilometres away. This is a costly habit with a simple alternative. If leaves were collected at the park depots and composted locally, the council could return rich soil to the same gardens that lose their leaves each year. Critics say composting needs space and attracts pests, but the depot at Millbrook has operated for six years without a single recorded complaint. Surely a practice that saves money, reduces transport and rebuilds soil deserves a fair trial in every suburb.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P02-Q5', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which new fact would most weaken the writer’s argument?', '["Leaves contain nutrients that plants can use.","A trial in a crowded suburb produced frequent pest problems.","The Millbrook depot is popular with visitors.","The trucks used for collection are replaced every few years."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Evidence of pest problems elsewhere would undermine the claim that depots work in every suburb.', 'Every autumn, our town sweeps thousands of tonnes of fallen leaves into trucks and drives them to a landfill site forty kilometres away. This is a costly habit with a simple alternative. If leaves were collected at the park depots and composted locally, the council could return rich soil to the same gardens that lose their leaves each year. Critics say composting needs space and attracts pests, but the depot at Millbrook has operated for six years without a single recorded complaint. Surely a practice that saves money, reduces transport and rebuilds soil deserves a fair trial in every suburb.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P03-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'What led to the construction of the lighthouse?', '["The government wanted to attract tourists.","Builders discovered local granite on the headland.","Several ships were wrecked on the reef.","Automatic lights became available."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The passage says the lighthouse was lit after three ships were wrecked in one winter.', 'The lighthouse at Cape Arden was first lit in 1874, after three ships were wrecked on the reef within a single winter. Its builders cut the tower from local granite, hauling each block up the headland by horse and sledge. For almost a century, keepers climbed 142 steps each evening to trim the lamp and wind the clockwork that turned the lens. When automatic lights arrived in 1969, the keepers’ cottages were abandoned. Today the tower is a museum, and visitors can see the original lens, whose glass prisms still gather a small flame’s glow into a beam that can be seen from thirty kilometres away.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P03-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which reason best explains why the cottages were abandoned in 1969?', '["The cottages were badly damaged in a severe winter storm.","The tower had been turned into a museum.","The granite blocks were reused elsewhere.","Automatic lights meant keepers were no longer needed."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The passage links the abandonment of the cottages to the arrival of automatic lights.', 'The lighthouse at Cape Arden was first lit in 1874, after three ships were wrecked on the reef within a single winter. Its builders cut the tower from local granite, hauling each block up the headland by horse and sledge. For almost a century, keepers climbed 142 steps each evening to trim the lamp and wind the clockwork that turned the lens. When automatic lights arrived in 1969, the keepers’ cottages were abandoned. Today the tower is a museum, and visitors can see the original lens, whose glass prisms still gather a small flame’s glow into a beam that can be seen from thirty kilometres away.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P03-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'The detail about hauling blocks “by horse and sledge” mainly shows that', '["building the tower demanded great physical effort","horses were faster than modern machinery","the builders disliked working with the local granite","the headland was flat and easy to cross"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Moving heavy stone uphill without machines required effort and persistence.', 'The lighthouse at Cape Arden was first lit in 1874, after three ships were wrecked on the reef within a single winter. Its builders cut the tower from local granite, hauling each block up the headland by horse and sledge. For almost a century, keepers climbed 142 steps each evening to trim the lamp and wind the clockwork that turned the lens. When automatic lights arrived in 1969, the keepers’ cottages were abandoned. Today the tower is a museum, and visitors can see the original lens, whose glass prisms still gather a small flame’s glow into a beam that can be seen from thirty kilometres away.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P03-Q4', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which statement is supported by the final sentence?', '["The lens was removed from the tower when it was automated.","The lens turns a small flame into a far-reaching beam.","The beam can be seen only from inside the museum.","The lens needs electricity to produce its beam."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sentence says the prisms gather a small flame’s glow into a beam visible from thirty kilometres.', 'The lighthouse at Cape Arden was first lit in 1874, after three ships were wrecked on the reef within a single winter. Its builders cut the tower from local granite, hauling each block up the headland by horse and sledge. For almost a century, keepers climbed 142 steps each evening to trim the lamp and wind the clockwork that turned the lens. When automatic lights arrived in 1969, the keepers’ cottages were abandoned. Today the tower is a museum, and visitors can see the original lens, whose glass prisms still gather a small flame’s glow into a beam that can be seen from thirty kilometres away.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P03-Q5', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the main purpose of the passage?', '["To persuade readers to train as lighthouse keepers today","To compare granite with other building materials","To outline the history and importance of the lighthouse","To explain how sailors navigate using clockwork"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The passage traces the lighthouse from its origin to its use as a museum.', 'The lighthouse at Cape Arden was first lit in 1874, after three ships were wrecked on the reef within a single winter. Its builders cut the tower from local granite, hauling each block up the headland by horse and sledge. For almost a century, keepers climbed 142 steps each evening to trim the lamp and wind the clockwork that turned the lens. When automatic lights arrived in 1969, the keepers’ cottages were abandoned. Today the tower is a museum, and visitors can see the original lens, whose glass prisms still gather a small flame’s glow into a beam that can be seen from thirty kilometres away.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P04-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'What was the total number of visitors in 2023?', '["85 thousand","105 thousand","31 thousand","95 thousand"]'::jsonb, 3, '18 + 24 + 31 + 22 = 95 thousand.', 'The Harbour Museum recorded the following visitor numbers, in thousands. In 2023: January–March 18, April–June 24, July–September 31, October–December 22. In 2024: January–March 21, April–June 27, July–September 29, October–December 35. A new maritime gallery opened in October 2024.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P04-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'In which quarter did visitor numbers fall from 2023 to 2024?', '["July–September","January–March","April–June","October–December"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The July–September figure fell from 31 thousand to 29 thousand. All other quarters rose.', 'The Harbour Museum recorded the following visitor numbers, in thousands. In 2023: January–March 18, April–June 24, July–September 31, October–December 22. In 2024: January–March 21, April–June 27, July–September 29, October–December 35. A new maritime gallery opened in October 2024.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P04-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'By how many thousand did the October–December quarter increase from 2023 to 2024?', '["7","13","10","35"]'::jsonb, 1, '35 − 22 = 13 thousand.', 'The Harbour Museum recorded the following visitor numbers, in thousands. In 2023: January–March 18, April–June 24, July–September 31, October–December 22. In 2024: January–March 21, April–June 27, July–September 29, October–December 35. A new maritime gallery opened in October 2024.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P04-Q4', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which explanation is best supported by the data?', '["The new gallery caused visitor numbers to fall in every quarter of the year.","Summer was the busiest quarter in both years.","The new gallery may have helped lift visits in the final quarter.","Visitor numbers were identical in 2023 and 2024."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The largest increase came in the quarter the gallery opened, so it may have played a part, although the data cannot prove it.', 'The Harbour Museum recorded the following visitor numbers, in thousands. In 2023: January–March 18, April–June 24, July–September 31, October–December 22. In 2024: January–March 21, April–June 27, July–September 29, October–December 35. A new maritime gallery opened in October 2024.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P04-Q5', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which statement about 2024 is correct?', '["The busiest quarter was July–September.","The quietest quarter was April–June.","Every quarter exceeded 30 thousand visitors.","The busiest quarter was October–December."]'::jsonb, 3, 'October–December had 35 thousand visitors, more than any other quarter in 2024.', 'The Harbour Museum recorded the following visitor numbers, in thousands. In 2023: January–March 18, April–June 24, July–September 31, October–December 22. In 2024: January–March 21, April–June 27, July–September 29, October–December 35. A new maritime gallery opened in October 2024.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P05-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'Who is the advertisement most likely aimed at?', '["People wanting a quiet break from busy city life","Astronomers who need laboratory equipment","Families looking for cinema nights and arcades","Business travellers who need reliable phone coverage"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ad stresses leaving the city behind, with no screens or signal.', 'SLEEP UNDER REAL STARS. Leave the city glow behind. At Wren Ridge Retreat, our domed cabins have no televisions, no streetlights and no phone signal, only a sky so full of stars that you will forget your phone exists. More than 2,000 guests have returned for a second stay. Book before the end of the month and receive a free sunrise breakfast hamper.* *Offer applies to midweek stays only and cannot be combined with other discounts.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P05-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which technique is used in “a sky so full of stars that you will forget your phone exists”?', '["A statistic taken from independent research","Exaggeration to create an appealing image","A direct quotation from an expert","A warning about possible danger"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The claim is an exaggeration that makes the stargazing experience sound irresistible.', 'SLEEP UNDER REAL STARS. Leave the city glow behind. At Wren Ridge Retreat, our domed cabins have no televisions, no streetlights and no phone signal, only a sky so full of stars that you will forget your phone exists. More than 2,000 guests have returned for a second stay. Book before the end of the month and receive a free sunrise breakfast hamper.* *Offer applies to midweek stays only and cannot be combined with other discounts.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P05-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the purpose of the information marked with an asterisk?', '["To praise the cabins even more","To list the rules guests must follow","To state limits on the offer","To thank guests who have returned"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The small print explains the conditions that apply to the free hamper.', 'SLEEP UNDER REAL STARS. Leave the city glow behind. At Wren Ridge Retreat, our domed cabins have no televisions, no streetlights and no phone signal, only a sky so full of stars that you will forget your phone exists. More than 2,000 guests have returned for a second stay. Book before the end of the month and receive a free sunrise breakfast hamper.* *Offer applies to midweek stays only and cannot be combined with other discounts.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P05-Q4', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which claim in the advertisement is supported by a figure?', '["The retreat has the clearest sky in the country.","Every cabin has a view of the sunrise.","Phones never work anywhere in the region.","Many guests have stayed more than once."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ad gives a number, 2,000 returning guests, only for the claim about repeat stays.', 'SLEEP UNDER REAL STARS. Leave the city glow behind. At Wren Ridge Retreat, our domed cabins have no televisions, no streetlights and no phone signal, only a sky so full of stars that you will forget your phone exists. More than 2,000 guests have returned for a second stay. Book before the end of the month and receive a free sunrise breakfast hamper.* *Offer applies to midweek stays only and cannot be combined with other discounts.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P05-Q5', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the effect of “Book before the end of the month”?', '["It creates urgency so readers decide quickly.","It explains when the stars are visible.","It announces a new type of cabin for the summer.","It warns that the retreat will then close."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A deadline pushes readers to act soon rather than delay.', 'SLEEP UNDER REAL STARS. Leave the city glow behind. At Wren Ridge Retreat, our domed cabins have no televisions, no streetlights and no phone signal, only a sky so full of stars that you will forget your phone exists. More than 2,000 guests have returned for a second stay. Book before the end of the month and receive a free sunrise breakfast hamper.* *Offer applies to midweek stays only and cannot be combined with other discounts.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P06-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'What does Theo’s reaction to Mara’s suggestion reveal?', '["He enjoys asking strangers for directions.","He is reluctant to admit that he might be lost.","He wants to leave the map behind.","He believes Mara is a better navigator than he is."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Theo insists everything is fine, which suggests he does not want to admit a mistake.', 'MARA: (not looking up from the map) We could just ask someone.
THEO: We’re fine. I know where the road splits.
MARA: That was an hour ago.
THEO: (folding his arms) The road has probably moved.
MARA: Roads don’t move, Theo.
THEO: Then it’s hiding. (A long pause. A car passes in the distance.)
MARA: I’m not saying you’re wrong. I’m saying the sun is going down.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P06'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P06-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the effect of Theo’s remark, “The road has probably moved”?', '["It gives Mara accurate directions.","It shows he is angry at the passing car.","It uses humour to avoid admitting a mistake.","It proves that roads change position during the day."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The joke deflects attention from his error instead of facing it.', 'MARA: (not looking up from the map) We could just ask someone.
THEO: We’re fine. I know where the road splits.
MARA: That was an hour ago.
THEO: (folding his arms) The road has probably moved.
MARA: Roads don’t move, Theo.
THEO: Then it’s hiding. (A long pause. A car passes in the distance.)
MARA: I’m not saying you’re wrong. I’m saying the sun is going down.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P06'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P06-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'The stage direction “A long pause” most likely suggests', '["that the argument has been settled between them","that Mara has fallen asleep","that the passing car has stopped to help","tension as both characters consider what to do"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The silence follows Theo’s joke and gives the moment weight.', 'MARA: (not looking up from the map) We could just ask someone.
THEO: We’re fine. I know where the road splits.
MARA: That was an hour ago.
THEO: (folding his arms) The road has probably moved.
