  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P07-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'According to the teacher, why has attendance fallen?', '["The teacher has closed the school for the whole season.","Parents disapprove of the Education Board.","Children are needed to work in the fields at harvest.","The pupils have no textbooks to use."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The letter says the harvest has begun and families need their children’s labour.', 'Marlow Creek, 14 March 1886. To the Education Board. I write to report that attendance at my school has fallen from thirty-one pupils in February to nineteen this week. The cause is not idleness. Harvest has begun, and every family needs the labour of its children in the fields from dawn until the light fails. I do not condemn these parents; I have seen how little the land gives them. I respectfully ask the Board to consider a shortened school year in harvest season rather than punish families already struggling. Yours faithfully, E. Hartley, Teacher.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P07-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the teacher’s attitude towards the parents?', '["Angry and openly critical","Indifferent and uninterested in them","Amused and dismissive","Sympathetic and understanding"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The teacher says, “I do not condemn these parents,” and acknowledges their hardship.', 'Marlow Creek, 14 March 1886. To the Education Board. I write to report that attendance at my school has fallen from thirty-one pupils in February to nineteen this week. The cause is not idleness. Harvest has begun, and every family needs the labour of its children in the fields from dawn until the light fails. I do not condemn these parents; I have seen how little the land gives them. I respectfully ask the Board to consider a shortened school year in harvest season rather than punish families already struggling. Yours faithfully, E. Hartley, Teacher.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P07-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the main purpose of the letter?', '["To ask the Board to adjust school rules at harvest time","To announce that the school will close permanently this year","To request that more pupils be enrolled","To complain about the teacher’s salary"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The teacher asks for a shortened school year in harvest season.', 'Marlow Creek, 14 March 1886. To the Education Board. I write to report that attendance at my school has fallen from thirty-one pupils in February to nineteen this week. The cause is not idleness. Harvest has begun, and every family needs the labour of its children in the fields from dawn until the light fails. I do not condemn these parents; I have seen how little the land gives them. I respectfully ask the Board to consider a shortened school year in harvest season rather than punish families already struggling. Yours faithfully, E. Hartley, Teacher.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P07-Q4', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What does the phrase “how little the land gives them” suggest about the families?', '["They own very large and profitable farms.","They earn only a limited living from farming.","They are unwilling to farm their land.","They live too far from the school to attend."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The phrase implies that farming brings the families little return.', 'Marlow Creek, 14 March 1886. To the Education Board. I write to report that attendance at my school has fallen from thirty-one pupils in February to nineteen this week. The cause is not idleness. Harvest has begun, and every family needs the labour of its children in the fields from dawn until the light fails. I do not condemn these parents; I have seen how little the land gives them. I respectfully ask the Board to consider a shortened school year in harvest season rather than punish families already struggling. Yours faithfully, E. Hartley, Teacher.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P07-Q5', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which feature makes this letter useful to a historian?', '["It lists the name and age of every pupil.","It describes detailed plans for a new school building in town.","It shows how farming needs affected schooling in the 1880s.","It records the Board’s reply to the request."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The letter is first-hand evidence of the link between farm work and school attendance, although it reflects only one teacher’s view.', 'Marlow Creek, 14 March 1886. To the Education Board. I write to report that attendance at my school has fallen from thirty-one pupils in February to nineteen this week. The cause is not idleness. Harvest has begun, and every family needs the labour of its children in the fields from dawn until the light fails. I do not condemn these parents; I have seen how little the land gives them. I respectfully ask the Board to consider a shortened school year in harvest season rather than punish families already struggling. Yours faithfully, E. Hartley, Teacher.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P08-Q1', 'humanities', 'medium', 55, 'The streetlights are compared to “a slow sentence being written.” What does this suggest?', '["The lights are broken and flicker constantly.","The street is covered in messages written in chalk.","The lights flash on and off in a secret code.","The lights come on gradually, one after another."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A sentence is built word by word, just as the lights appear one at a time.', 'By the time I reached the bottom of the hill, the streetlights had flickered on one by one, like a slow sentence being written across the dusk. The bakery was closing; its window, still warm, fogged at the edges. A man swept the footpath with unhurried strokes, as though the evening were something to be tidied gently rather than hurried along. I slowed down. For the first time all day, there was nowhere I needed to be faster than the light.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P08-Q2', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What does the phrase “something to be tidied gently” suggest about the man?', '["He works calmly and without rushing.","He is angry about the mess on the footpath.","He dislikes his job and wants to finish.","He is hurrying to close the shop."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sweeping with “unhurried strokes” shows a calm, careful manner.', 'By the time I reached the bottom of the hill, the streetlights had flickered on one by one, like a slow sentence being written across the dusk. The bakery was closing; its window, still warm, fogged at the edges. A man swept the footpath with unhurried strokes, as though the evening were something to be tidied gently rather than hurried along. I slowed down. For the first time all day, there was nowhere I needed to be faster than the light.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P08-Q3', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'Which word best describes the narrator’s mood at the end?', '["Anxious","Peaceful","Resentful","Bewildered"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The narrator slows down and feels free of pressure.', 'By the time I reached the bottom of the hill, the streetlights had flickered on one by one, like a slow sentence being written across the dusk. The bakery was closing; its window, still warm, fogged at the edges. A man swept the footpath with unhurried strokes, as though the evening were something to be tidied gently rather than hurried along. I slowed down. For the first time all day, there was nowhere I needed to be faster than the light.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P08-Q4', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What makes the narrator slow down?', '["The hill has become too steep for the narrator to climb.","The narrator is afraid of the dark.","The calm scene makes hurrying seem unnecessary.","The bakery is about to open."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The peaceful evening scene shows that there is no need to rush.', 'By the time I reached the bottom of the hill, the streetlights had flickered on one by one, like a slow sentence being written across the dusk. The bakery was closing; its window, still warm, fogged at the edges. A man swept the footpath with unhurried strokes, as though the evening were something to be tidied gently rather than hurried along. I slowed down. For the first time all day, there was nowhere I needed to be faster than the light.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('HUM-BANK-N1-P08-Q5', 'humanities', 'hard', 60, 'What is the effect of the final sentence?', '["It shows the narrator is late for an important appointment.","It proves the streetlights are moving quickly.","It introduces a new character to the story.","It contrasts a usual rush with a rare sense of ease."]'::jsonb, 3, '“For the first time all day” sets the calm of this moment against a busy day.', 'By the time I reached the bottom of the hill, the streetlights had flickered on one by one, like a slow sentence being written across the dusk. The bakery was closing; its window, still warm, fogged at the edges. A man swept the footpath with unhurried strokes, as though the evening were something to be tidied gently rather than hurried along. I slowed down. For the first time all day, there was nowhere I needed to be faster than the light.', 'HUM-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P01-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How many cells are expected after 2 hours (120 minutes)?', '["4,000","2,000","8,000","3,000"]'::jsonb, 0, '120 minutes is 3 doublings: 500 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 4,000.', 'A microbiologist grows bacteria in a nutrient broth. The culture begins with 500 cells and the number of cells doubles every 40 minutes while nutrients are plentiful. The microbiologist records the cell count at regular intervals.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P01-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How many cells are expected after 4 hours (240 minutes)?', '["16,000","32,000","6,000","3,000"]'::jsonb, 1, '240 minutes is 6 doublings: 500 × 2⁶ = 32,000.', 'A microbiologist grows bacteria in a nutrient broth. The culture begins with 500 cells and the number of cells doubles every 40 minutes while nutrients are plentiful. The microbiologist records the cell count at regular intervals.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P01-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'After how many minutes will the culture first exceed 10,000 cells?', '["160","240","200","250"]'::jsonb, 2, 'After 160 minutes there are 8,000 cells, which is not enough. After 200 minutes (5 doublings) there are 16,000 cells.', 'A microbiologist grows bacteria in a nutrient broth. The culture begins with 500 cells and the number of cells doubles every 40 minutes while nutrients are plentiful. The microbiologist records the cell count at regular intervals.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P01'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P02-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How much electricity do the panels produce on a sunny day?', '["1.2 kWh","18 kWh","10.8 kWh","16.2 kWh"]'::jsonb, 1, '15 panels × 1.2 kWh = 18 kWh.', 'A household uses 18 kWh of electricity each day. Each rooftop solar panel produces 1.2 kWh on a sunny day. The household has 15 panels. On a cloudy day each panel produces only 60% of its sunny-day output. The household also has a battery that stores 13.5 kWh.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P02-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How much electricity do the panels produce on a cloudy day?', '["7.2 kWh","9.2 kWh","10.8 kWh","12.0 kWh"]'::jsonb, 2, '60% of 18 kWh = 10.8 kWh.', 'A household uses 18 kWh of electricity each day. Each rooftop solar panel produces 1.2 kWh on a sunny day. The household has 15 panels. On a cloudy day each panel produces only 60% of its sunny-day output. The household also has a battery that stores 13.5 kWh.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P02-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'On a cloudy day, how much electricity is the household short of its daily use?', '["10.8 kWh","8.2 kWh","6.0 kWh","7.2 kWh"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The shortfall is 18 − 10.8 = 7.2 kWh.', 'A household uses 18 kWh of electricity each day. Each rooftop solar panel produces 1.2 kWh on a sunny day. The household has 15 panels. On a cloudy day each panel produces only 60% of its sunny-day output. The household also has a battery that stores 13.5 kWh.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P02'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P03-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'Which variable is the independent variable in this investigation?', '["The period of the pendulum","The mass of the pendulum","The length of the pendulum","The starting angle"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The student deliberately changes the length and measures the period in response.', 'A student investigates how the length of a pendulum affects its period (the time for one full swing). The mass and the starting angle are kept the same. The results are: length 25 cm, period 1.0 s; length 50 cm, period 1.4 s; length 100 cm, period 2.0 s.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P03-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'The length is increased from 25 cm to 100 cm. What happens to the period?', '["It quadruples","It stays the same","It increases by 1.0 s","It doubles"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The length is multiplied by 4, but the period rises from 1.0 s to 2.0 s, which is multiplied by 2.', 'A student investigates how the length of a pendulum affects its period (the time for one full swing). The mass and the starting angle are kept the same. The results are: length 25 cm, period 1.0 s; length 50 cm, period 1.4 s; length 100 cm, period 2.0 s.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P03-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Which conclusion is best supported by the results?', '["Period increases with length, but not in direct proportion to it","Period is directly proportional to length","Period does not depend on length","Period decreases as length increases"]'::jsonb, 0, 'If period were proportional to length, 4 times the length would give 4 times the period. It gives only 2 times.', 'A student investigates how the length of a pendulum affects its period (the time for one full swing). The mass and the starting angle are kept the same. The results are: length 25 cm, period 1.0 s; length 50 cm, period 1.4 s; length 100 cm, period 2.0 s.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P03'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P04-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How far from the station is Train A when Train B departs?', '["120 km","160 km","40 km","80 km"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Train A has travelled for 1 hour at 80 km/h, so it is 80 km from the station.', 'Train A leaves a station at 10:00 and travels at a constant 80 km/h along a straight track. Train B leaves the same station on the same track at 11:00 and travels at a constant 120 km/h in the same direction. Neither train stops.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P04-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'At what time does Train B catch Train A?', '["13:00","12:00","12:30","14:00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Train B gains 120 − 80 = 40 km each hour, so the 80 km gap closes in 2 hours after 11:00.', 'Train A leaves a station at 10:00 and travels at a constant 80 km/h along a straight track. Train B leaves the same station on the same track at 11:00 and travels at a constant 120 km/h in the same direction. Neither train stops.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P04-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How far from the station are the trains when Train B catches Train A?', '["80 km","240 km","160 km","360 km"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Train B travels for 2 hours at 120 km/h: 240 km. Train A travels for 3 hours at 80 km/h: also 240 km.', 'Train A leaves a station at 10:00 and travels at a constant 80 km/h along a straight track. Train B leaves the same station on the same track at 11:00 and travels at a constant 120 km/h in the same direction. Neither train stops.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P04'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P05-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How much does the yield increase when fertiliser rises from 0 kg to 20 kg?', '["0.9 t/ha","3.0 t/ha","0.6 t/ha","1.5 t/ha"]'::jsonb, 0, '3.0 − 2.1 = 0.9 t/ha.', 'A farmer tests four amounts of fertiliser on identical plots of wheat. The yield is measured in tonnes per hectare. With 0 kg of fertiliser the yield is 2.1 t/ha; with 20 kg it is 3 t/ha; with 40 kg it is 3.6 t/ha; and with 60 kg it is 3.7 t/ha. Every plot received the same water and sunlight.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P05-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Which increase in fertiliser produced the smallest gain in yield?', '["From 0 kg to 20 kg","From 40 kg to 60 kg","From 20 kg to 40 kg","All increases produced the same gain"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gains are 0.9, 0.6 and 0.1 t/ha, so the last 20 kg added only 0.1 t/ha.', 'A farmer tests four amounts of fertiliser on identical plots of wheat. The yield is measured in tonnes per hectare. With 0 kg of fertiliser the yield is 2.1 t/ha; with 20 kg it is 3 t/ha; with 40 kg it is 3.6 t/ha; and with 60 kg it is 3.7 t/ha. Every plot received the same water and sunlight.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P05-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the percentage increase in yield from 0 kg to 60 kg of fertiliser (to the nearest whole number)?', '["1.6%","57%","76%","176%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The increase is 3.7 − 2.1 = 1.6 t/ha. 1.6 ÷ 2.1 × 100 ≈ 76%.', 'A farmer tests four amounts of fertiliser on identical plots of wheat. The yield is measured in tonnes per hectare. With 0 kg of fertiliser the yield is 2.1 t/ha; with 20 kg it is 3 t/ha; with 40 kg it is 3.6 t/ha; and with 60 kg it is 3.7 t/ha. Every plot received the same water and sunlight.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P05'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P06-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'What is the probability that one token drawn at random is blue?', '["3/7","3/10","1/3","3/5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'There are 3 blue tokens out of 10, so the probability is 3/10.', 'A bag contains 10 tokens: 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green. Tokens are identical apart from colour and are mixed thoroughly before each draw.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P06'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P06-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Two tokens are drawn without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?', '["1/4","1/5","2/9","5/18"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The first is red with probability 5/10, then 4 reds remain among 9 tokens: 5/10 × 4/9 = 20/90 = 2/9.', 'A bag contains 10 tokens: 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green. Tokens are identical apart from colour and are mixed thoroughly before each draw.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P06'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P06-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'A token is drawn, its colour is recorded and it is replaced. This is done 40 times. About how many red tokens are expected?', '["5","10","25","20"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each draw is red with probability 1/2, so the expected number in 40 draws is 20. The actual number may differ.', 'A bag contains 10 tokens: 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green. Tokens are identical apart from colour and are mixed thoroughly before each draw.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P06'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P07-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'Two huts are 4.6 cm apart on the map. How far apart are they on the ground?', '["11.5 km","0.115 km","1.15 km","4.6 km"]'::jsonb, 2, '4.6 × 25,000 = 115,000 cm = 1,150 m = 1.15 km.', 'A walking map has a scale of 1 : 25,000. This means 1 cm on the map represents 25,000 cm (250 m) on the ground.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P07-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'A track is 3 km long. How long is it on the map?', '["7.5 cm","120 cm","1.2 cm","12 cm"]'::jsonb, 3, '3 km = 300,000 cm. 300,000 ÷ 25,000 = 12 cm.', 'A walking map has a scale of 1 : 25,000. This means 1 cm on the map represents 25,000 cm (250 m) on the ground.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P07-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'A lake covers 4 cm² on the map. What is its real area?', '["25 hectares","1 hectare","100 hectares","250 hectares"]'::jsonb, 0, '1 cm represents 250 m, so 1 cm² represents 62,500 m². 4 cm² = 250,000 m² = 25 hectares.', 'A walking map has a scale of 1 : 25,000. This means 1 cm on the map represents 25,000 cm (250 m) on the ground.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P07'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P08-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How many moles of salt are in the original solution?', '["0.40 mol","0.16 mol","1.60 mol","0.10 mol"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Moles = concentration × volume = 0.40 mol/L × 0.25 L = 0.10 mol.', 'A chemist has 250 mL of a salt solution with a concentration of 0.40 mol/L. Concentration is the amount of dissolved salt (in moles) divided by the volume of solution (in litres). The chemist adds water to make the volume up to 1.0 L.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P08-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the concentration after the water is added to reach 1.0 L?', '["0.10 mol/L","0.40 mol/L","0.25 mol/L","0.05 mol/L"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Adding water does not change the 0.10 mol of salt. 0.10 mol ÷ 1.0 L = 0.10 mol/L.', 'A chemist has 250 mL of a salt solution with a concentration of 0.40 mol/L. Concentration is the amount of dissolved salt (in moles) divided by the volume of solution (in litres). The chemist adds water to make the volume up to 1.0 L.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P08-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What volume of the 0.40 mol/L solution is needed to make 500 mL of 0.08 mol/L solution by adding water?', '["80 mL","100 mL","250 mL","40 mL"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The final solution needs 0.08 × 0.5 = 0.04 mol. 0.04 ÷ 0.40 = 0.10 L = 100 mL.', 'A chemist has 250 mL of a salt solution with a concentration of 0.40 mol/L. Concentration is the amount of dissolved salt (in moles) divided by the volume of solution (in litres). The chemist adds water to make the volume up to 1.0 L.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P08'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P09-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How much energy does the old heater use each day?', '["6 kWh","2 kWh","3 kWh","1.8 kWh"]'::jsonb, 0, '2.0 kW × 3 h = 6 kWh.', 'An old heater uses 2.0 kW of power and is switched on for 3 hours each day. A new, more efficient heater provides the same warmth but uses 1.2 kW. Electricity costs $0.30 per kWh, and energy used in kWh equals power in kW multiplied by time in hours. The new heater costs $216.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P09-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What does it cost to run the old heater for one day?', '["$0.60","$1.80","$6.00","$0.72"]'::jsonb, 1, '6 kWh × $0.30 = $1.80.', 'An old heater uses 2.0 kW of power and is switched on for 3 hours each day. A new, more efficient heater provides the same warmth but uses 1.2 kW. Electricity costs $0.30 per kWh, and energy used in kWh equals power in kW multiplied by time in hours. The new heater costs $216.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P09-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How much money does the new heater save each day?', '["$0.80","$1.08","$0.72","$0.24"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The new heater saves 0.8 kW × 3 h = 2.4 kWh, and 2.4 × $0.30 = $0.72.', 'An old heater uses 2.0 kW of power and is switched on for 3 hours each day. A new, more efficient heater provides the same warmth but uses 1.2 kW. Electricity costs $0.30 per kWh, and energy used in kWh equals power in kW multiplied by time in hours. The new heater costs $216.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P09-Q4', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'After how many days will the savings equal the purchase price of the new heater?', '["120 days","270 days","216 days","300 days"]'::jsonb, 3, '$216 ÷ $0.72 per day = 300 days.', 'An old heater uses 2.0 kW of power and is switched on for 3 hours each day. A new, more efficient heater provides the same warmth but uses 1.2 kW. Electricity costs $0.30 per kWh, and energy used in kWh equals power in kW multiplied by time in hours. The new heater costs $216.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P10-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'Two plants with the genotype Tt are crossed. What is the probability that an offspring is short?', '["1/2","1/4","3/4","0"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An offspring is short only if it receives t from both parents: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4.', 'In a species of pea plant, tall (T) is dominant to short (t). A tall plant can have the genotype TT or Tt, and a short plant must have the genotype tt. Each parent passes on one of its two alleles to each offspring, and each allele is equally likely to be passed on.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P10'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P10-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'The cross Tt × Tt produces 80 offspring. About how many are expected to be tall?', '["20","40","60","80"]'::jsonb, 2, '3/4 of the offspring are expected to be tall: 80 × 3/4 = 60.', 'In a species of pea plant, tall (T) is dominant to short (t). A tall plant can have the genotype TT or Tt, and a short plant must have the genotype tt. Each parent passes on one of its two alleles to each offspring, and each allele is equally likely to be passed on.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P10'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P10-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'A tall plant of unknown genotype is crossed with a short plant, and every one of 12 offspring is tall. Which conclusion is best supported?', '["The tall parent is definitely Tt","The tall parent must be tt","No conclusion is possible","The tall parent is probably TT"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A Tt parent would give about half short offspring. Twelve tall offspring in a row makes TT very likely, although it is not absolutely certain.', 'In a species of pea plant, tall (T) is dominant to short (t). A tall plant can have the genotype TT or Tt, and a short plant must have the genotype tt. Each parent passes on one of its two alleles to each offspring, and each allele is equally likely to be passed on.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P10'),
  ('MS-BANK-N1-P10-Q4', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'A Tt plant is crossed with a short plant and produces 200 offspring. About how many are expected to be short?', '["100","50","150","200"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tt × tt gives Tt and tt equally often, so about half (100) are expected to be short.', 'In a species of pea plant, tall (T) is dominant to short (t). A tall plant can have the genotype TT or Tt, and a short plant must have the genotype tt. Each parent passes on one of its two alleles to each offspring, and each allele is equally likely to be passed on.', 'MATH-BANK-N1-P10'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P01-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'What mass of the isotope remains after 15 days?', '["100 g","200 g","400 g","50 g"]'::jsonb, 0, '15 days is 3 half-lives: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 g.', 'A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 5 days. This means that in every 5-day period, half of the radioactive atoms present at the start of that period decay into other elements. A laboratory begins with a 800 g sample of the isotope.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P01'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P01-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'After how many days will 50 g or less of the isotope remain?', '["15 days","20 days","25 days","10 days"]'::jsonb, 1, 'After 15 days 100 g remains, and after 20 days (4 half-lives) 50 g remains.', 'A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 5 days. This means that in every 5-day period, half of the radioactive atoms present at the start of that period decay into other elements. A laboratory begins with a 800 g sample of the isotope.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P01'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P01-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Which statement about the decay is correct?', '["The same mass of the sample decays in each 5-day period","The sample is completely gone after 40 days","The same fraction of the remaining sample decays in each 5-day period","The decay speeds up as the sample becomes smaller"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Half of whatever is left decays each period, so the mass lost shrinks over time (400 g, then 200 g, then 100 g), and the sample never reaches exactly zero.', 'A radioactive isotope has a half-life of 5 days. This means that in every 5-day period, half of the radioactive atoms present at the start of that period decay into other elements. A laboratory begins with a 800 g sample of the isotope.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P01'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P02-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How much fuel will the family car use on the 480 km trip?', '["64 L","36 L","3.6 L","45 L"]'::jsonb, 1, '480 ÷ 100 = 4.8 lots of 100 km, and 4.8 × 7.5 L = 36 L.', 'A family car uses 7.5 litres of fuel per 100 km. Fuel costs $2.10 per litre. The family is planning a 480 km holiday drive. A hybrid car uses 4.5 litres per 100 km.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P02'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P02-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What will the fuel for the trip cost in the family car?', '["$7.56","$63.00","$75.60","$134.40"]'::jsonb, 2, '36 L × $2.10 per litre = $75.60.', 'A family car uses 7.5 litres of fuel per 100 km. Fuel costs $2.10 per litre. The family is planning a 480 km holiday drive. A hybrid car uses 4.5 litres per 100 km.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P02'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P02-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How much less fuel would the hybrid car use on the same trip?', '["21.6 L","36 L","3.0 L","14.4 L"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The hybrid uses 3.0 L less per 100 km, and 4.8 × 3.0 L = 14.4 L.', 'A family car uses 7.5 litres of fuel per 100 km. Fuel costs $2.10 per litre. The family is planning a 480 km holiday drive. A hybrid car uses 4.5 litres per 100 km.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P02'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P03-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'What is the price after the 20% increase?', '["$220","$200","$240","$180"]'::jsonb, 2, '20% of $200 is $40, so the new price is $240.', 'A shop prices a jacket at $200. The price is first increased by 20%, and then the new price is reduced by 20%. A customer is trying to work out whether the two changes cancel each other out.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P03'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P03-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the final price after the 20% reduction is applied to the new price?', '["$200","$180","$160","$192"]'::jsonb, 3, '20% of $240 is $48, and $240 − $48 = $192.', 'A shop prices a jacket at $200. The price is first increased by 20%, and then the new price is reduced by 20%. A customer is trying to work out whether the two changes cancel each other out.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P03'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P03-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the overall percentage change from the original price?', '["4% decrease","No change","20% decrease","4% increase"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The final price is $8 below $200, which is 8 ÷ 200 = 4%. The two percentages apply to different amounts, so they do not cancel.', 'A shop prices a jacket at $200. The price is first increased by 20%, and then the new price is reduced by 20%. A customer is trying to work out whether the two changes cancel each other out.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P03'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P04-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How many litres of blue paint are in the original 20 L mixture?', '["8 L","10 L","6 L","12 L"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Blue is 3 of every 5 parts: 3/5 × 20 L = 12 L.', 'A decorator mixes blue and white paint in the ratio 3 : 2 (blue : white) to make 20 litres of a pale colour. After mixing, the decorator decides the colour is too dark and adds 5 more litres of white paint.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P04'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P04-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the ratio of blue to white paint after the extra white is added?', '["12 : 13","3 : 2","12 : 5","13 : 12"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The original mixture has 12 L blue and 8 L white. After adding 5 L of white there are 12 L of blue and 13 L of white.', 'A decorator mixes blue and white paint in the ratio 3 : 2 (blue : white) to make 20 litres of a pale colour. After mixing, the decorator decides the colour is too dark and adds 5 more litres of white paint.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P04'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P04-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'After adding the 5 L of white, how much extra blue paint must be added to restore the 3 : 2 ratio?', '["5 L","7.5 L","6.5 L","13 L"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With 13 L of white, the ratio needs 13 × 3/2 = 19.5 L of blue. There is 12 L, so 7.5 L must be added.', 'A decorator mixes blue and white paint in the ratio 3 : 2 (blue : white) to make 20 litres of a pale colour. After mixing, the decorator decides the colour is too dark and adds 5 more litres of white paint.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P04'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P05-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'By how many degrees does the temperature fall during the first 5 minutes?', '["28 °C","15 °C","90 °C","58 °C"]'::jsonb, 0, '90 − 62 = 28 °C.', 'A cup of hot water is left to cool in a room at 22 °C. Its temperature is recorded every 5 minutes: 0 min, 90 °C; 5 min, 62 °C; 10 min, 47 °C; 15 min, 38 °C; 20 min, 32 °C.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P05'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P05-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'During which 5-minute interval does the temperature fall by the smallest amount?', '["0–5 minutes","15–20 minutes","5–10 minutes","10–15 minutes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The falls are 28, 15, 9 and 6 °C, so the smallest fall is between 15 and 20 minutes.', 'A cup of hot water is left to cool in a room at 22 °C. Its temperature is recorded every 5 minutes: 0 min, 90 °C; 5 min, 62 °C; 10 min, 47 °C; 15 min, 38 °C; 20 min, 32 °C.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P05'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P05-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Which conclusion is best supported by the results?', '["The water cools at a constant rate","The water cools more quickly as it gets closer to room temperature","The water cools more slowly as it gets closer to room temperature","The water will eventually fall below room temperature"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The falls get smaller each interval as the temperature approaches 22 °C. Water cooling in a room does not drop below the room''s temperature.', 'A cup of hot water is left to cool in a room at 22 °C. Its temperature is recorded every 5 minutes: 0 min, 90 °C; 5 min, 62 °C; 10 min, 47 °C; 15 min, 38 °C; 20 min, 32 °C.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P05'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P06-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'What is the area of the garden?', '["18.84 m²","28.26 m²","9.42 m²","56.52 m²"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Area = π × r² = 3.14 × 3 × 3 = 28.26 m².', 'A circular garden has a radius of 3 m. A path 1 m wide is built all the way around the garden, so the outer edge of the path is also a circle. Use π = 3.14 for all calculations.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P06'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P06-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the area enclosed by the outer edge of the path?', '["37.68 m²","28.26 m²","50.24 m²","12.56 m²"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The outer circle has radius 3 + 1 = 4 m, so its area is 3.14 × 4 × 4 = 50.24 m².', 'A circular garden has a radius of 3 m. A path 1 m wide is built all the way around the garden, so the outer edge of the path is also a circle. Use π = 3.14 for all calculations.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P06'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P06-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the area of the path itself?', '["50.24 m²","28.26 m²","12.56 m²","21.98 m²"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Path area = 50.24 − 28.26 = 21.98 m².', 'A circular garden has a radius of 3 m. A path 1 m wide is built all the way around the garden, so the outer edge of the path is also a circle. Use π = 3.14 for all calculations.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P06'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P07-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'What is the balance after 2 years?', '["$5,400.00","$5,208.00","$5,408.00","$5,416.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Year 1: 5,000 × 1.04 = 5,200. Year 2: 5,200 × 1.04 = 5,408.', 'A student deposits $5,000 in a savings account that pays 4% interest per year. At the end of each year the interest is added to the balance, and the next year''s interest is calculated on the new, larger balance. The student makes no other deposits or withdrawals.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P07'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P07-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How much interest has been earned in total after 3 years?', '["$600.00","$5,624.32","$208.00","$624.32"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The balance after 3 years is 5,408 × 1.04 = 5,624.32, so the interest earned is 624.32.', 'A student deposits $5,000 in a savings account that pays 4% interest per year. At the end of each year the interest is added to the balance, and the next year''s interest is calculated on the new, larger balance. The student makes no other deposits or withdrawals.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P07'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P07-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How much more interest does this account earn over 3 years than simple interest of 4% on the original amount?', '["$24.32","$12.16","$48.64","$0.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Simple interest over 3 years is 3 × 200 = 600. The difference is 624.32 − 600 = 24.32.', 'A student deposits $5,000 in a savings account that pays 4% interest per year. At the end of each year the interest is added to the balance, and the next year''s interest is calculated on the new, larger balance. The student makes no other deposits or withdrawals.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P07'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P08-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'What is the density of the metal?', '["0.37 g/cm³","1.0 g/cm³","340 g/cm³","2.7 g/cm³"]'::jsonb, 3, '540 g ÷ 200 cm³ = 2.7 g/cm³.', 'A metal block has a mass of 540 g and a volume of 200 cm³. Density is mass divided by volume. Water has a density of 1.0 g/cm³. The block is lowered into a tank of water that has a flat base with an area of 400 cm².', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P08'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P08-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'What is the mass of a 50 cm³ piece of the same metal?', '["135 g","27 g","2.7 g","540 g"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mass = density × volume = 2.7 × 50 = 135 g.', 'A metal block has a mass of 540 g and a volume of 200 cm³. Density is mass divided by volume. Water has a density of 1.0 g/cm³. The block is lowered into a tank of water that has a flat base with an area of 400 cm².', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P08'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P08-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'The block is completely submerged. By how much does the water level in the tank rise?', '["2.0 cm","0.5 cm","1.35 cm","5.0 cm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The water rises by the block''s volume divided by the base area: 200 ÷ 400 = 0.5 cm.', 'A metal block has a mass of 540 g and a volume of 200 cm³. Density is mass divided by volume. Water has a density of 1.0 g/cm³. The block is lowered into a tank of water that has a flat base with an area of 400 cm².', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P08'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P09-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'How many different 3-digit codes are possible?', '["1,000","30","100","900"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each of the 3 positions has 10 choices: 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000.', 'A digital lock opens with a code made of digits from 0 to 9. Unless a question says otherwise, a digit may be used more than once in a code.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P09-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How many different 3-digit codes are possible if no digit may be repeated?', '["1,000","720","504","27"]'::jsonb, 1, 'There are 10 choices for the first digit, 9 for the second and 8 for the third: 10 × 9 × 8 = 720.', 'A digital lock opens with a code made of digits from 0 to 9. Unless a question says otherwise, a digit may be used more than once in a code.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P09-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How many different 4-digit codes are possible if digits may be repeated?', '["40","1,000","10,000","5,040"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each of the 4 positions has 10 choices: 10⁴ = 10,000.', 'A digital lock opens with a code made of digits from 0 to 9. Unless a question says otherwise, a digit may be used more than once in a code.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P09-Q4', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Someone guesses a 3-digit code at random in one attempt. What is the probability that the guess is correct?', '["1/100","1/999","3/10","1/1000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'There are 1,000 possible codes and exactly one is correct, so the probability is 1/1000.', 'A digital lock opens with a code made of digits from 0 to 9. Unless a question says otherwise, a digit may be used more than once in a code.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P09'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P10-Q1', 'mathematics_science', 'medium', 55, 'What is the weight of the box?', '["20 N","200 N","2 N","2,000 N"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Weight = mass × 10 N/kg = 20 × 10 = 200 N.', 'A 20 kg box is lifted onto a platform 1.5 m above the ground. A worker can either lift it straight up or slide it up a smooth ramp that is 5 m long and reaches the same platform. Take the force of gravity on each kilogram to be 10 N. Work done is force multiplied by the distance moved in the direction of the force.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P10'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P10-Q2', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'How much work is done lifting the box straight up 1.5 m?', '["200 J","30 J","300 J","3,000 J"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Work = 200 N × 1.5 m = 300 J.', 'A 20 kg box is lifted onto a platform 1.5 m above the ground. A worker can either lift it straight up or slide it up a smooth ramp that is 5 m long and reaches the same platform. Take the force of gravity on each kilogram to be 10 N. Work done is force multiplied by the distance moved in the direction of the force.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P10'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P10-Q3', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Ignoring friction, what force is needed to push the box up the 5 m ramp?', '["300 N","40 N","15 N","60 N"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same 300 J of work is done over 5 m, so the force is 300 ÷ 5 = 60 N.', 'A 20 kg box is lifted onto a platform 1.5 m above the ground. A worker can either lift it straight up or slide it up a smooth ramp that is 5 m long and reaches the same platform. Take the force of gravity on each kilogram to be 10 N. Work done is force multiplied by the distance moved in the direction of the force.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P10'),
  ('MS-BANK-N2-P10-Q4', 'mathematics_science', 'hard', 60, 'Why would the force needed on a real ramp be greater than the ideal value?', '["Friction between the box and the ramp means extra work must be done","Gravity is stronger on a ramp than in the air","The box gains mass while it slides","Work is not conserved when a ramp is used"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Friction opposes the motion, so additional force is needed beyond the 60 N that would lift the box without friction.', 'A 20 kg box is lifted onto a platform 1.5 m above the ground. A worker can either lift it straight up or slide it up a smooth ramp that is 5 m long and reaches the same platform. Take the force of gravity on each kilogram to be 10 N. Work done is force multiplied by the distance moved in the direction of the force.', 'MATH-BANK-N2-P10');

do $$
declare
  options_type text;
begin
  select data_type into options_type
  from information_schema.columns
  where table_schema = 'public'
    and table_name = 'generated_questions'
    and column_name = 'answer_options';

  if options_type = 'jsonb' then
    insert into public.generated_questions (
      id, session_id, ip_hash, year_level, section, difficulty, time, question_text,
      answer_options, correct_answer, explanation, passage, stimulus_group, stimulus_image, is_bank
    )
    select r.id, null, md5('question-bank-content|' || r.id), '10', r.section, r.difficulty, r.time, r.question_text,
           r.answer_options, r.correct_answer, r.explanation, r.passage, r.stimulus_group, null, true
    from new_question_bank_rows r
    where not exists (select 1 from public.generated_questions e where e.id = r.id);
  elsif options_type = 'json' then
    insert into public.generated_questions (
      id, session_id, ip_hash, year_level, section, difficulty, time, question_text,
      answer_options, correct_answer, explanation, passage, stimulus_group, stimulus_image, is_bank
    )
    select r.id, null, md5('question-bank-content|' || r.id), '10', r.section, r.difficulty, r.time, r.question_text,
           r.answer_options::json, r.correct_answer, r.explanation, r.passage, r.stimulus_group, null, true
    from new_question_bank_rows r
    where not exists (select 1 from public.generated_questions e where e.id = r.id);
  elsif options_type = 'ARRAY' then
    insert into public.generated_questions (
      id, session_id, ip_hash, year_level, section, difficulty, time, question_text,
      answer_options, correct_answer, explanation, passage, stimulus_group, stimulus_image, is_bank
    )
    select r.id, null, md5('question-bank-content|' || r.id), '10', r.section, r.difficulty, r.time, r.question_text,
           array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(r.answer_options)), r.correct_answer, r.explanation, r.passage, r.stimulus_group, null, true
    from new_question_bank_rows r
    where not exists (select 1 from public.generated_questions e where e.id = r.id);
  else
    raise exception 'Unsupported answer_options column type: %', coalesce(options_type, 'column not found');
  end if;
end
$$;

drop table if exists pg_temp.new_question_bank_rows;

insert into public.writing_topics (id, task_slot, topic, active)
values
  ('WT-1-05', 1, 'The things we throw away can tell a story about the people who owned them.', true),
  ('WT-1-06', 1, 'A place can change you even if you only stay there for a short time.', true),
  ('WT-1-07', 1, 'Sometimes the quietest person in the room notices the most.', true),
  ('WT-1-08', 1, 'Not every failure is the opposite of success.', true),
  ('WT-1-09', 1, 'Two people can look at the same thing and see different truths.', true),
  ('WT-1-10', 1, 'A skill learned for one purpose can turn out to matter for another.', true),
  ('WT-1-11', 1, 'The first impression is rarely the whole picture.', true),
  ('WT-1-12', 1, 'Some memories grow clearer with time, while others fade away.', true),
  ('WT-1-13', 1, 'A journey is not always measured in distance.', true),
  ('WT-1-14', 1, 'Surprise can be a kind of teacher.', true),
  ('WT-1-15', 1, 'What we choose to keep says something about what we value.', true),
  ('WT-1-16', 1, 'Courage does not always look like courage from the outside.', true),
  ('WT-2-05', 2, 'A promise matters most when it is hard to keep.', true),
  ('WT-2-06', 2, 'Advice is easy to give and much harder to follow.', true),
  ('WT-2-07', 2, 'Silence can be a form of respect, or a way of avoiding the truth.', true),
  ('WT-2-08', 2, 'Fairness and equality are not always the same thing.', true),
  ('WT-2-09', 2, 'A tradition lasts only while people find a reason to keep it.', true),
  ('WT-2-10', 2, 'Asking a question can be braver than giving an answer.', true),
  ('WT-2-11', 2, 'Technology can bring people together and keep them apart at the same time.', true),
  ('WT-2-12', 2, 'It is sometimes necessary to bend a small rule in order to honour a larger one.', true),
  ('WT-2-13', 2, 'A good argument changes the speaker as much as the listener.', true),
  ('WT-2-14', 2, 'Time spent waiting is never entirely wasted.', true),
  ('WT-2-15', 2, 'Trust is built slowly and lost quickly.', true),
  ('WT-2-16', 2, 'The stories we are told as children shape the adults we become.', true)
on conflict (id) do nothing;